Here's an example of how to use it:

```latex
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline
Cell 1 & Cell 2 \\ \cline{2-2}
Line 1 of Cell 2 & Line 2 of Cell 2 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
```

In this example, the `\\` command is used to create a line break within the second cell, allowing the text to span two lines.

You can also use the `\\` command to create a line break within a cell that spans multiple rows, using the `\cline` command to draw a horizontal line across the cell. The syntax for this is:

```latex
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline
Cell 1 & Cell 2 \\ \cline{2-2}
Line 1 of Cell 2 & Line 2 of Cell 2 \\
\cline{2-2}
Line 3 of Cell 2 & Line 4 of Cell 2 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
```

In this example, the `\cline{2-2}` command draws a horizontal line across the second column, spanning the two rows.

Note that the `\\` command can also be used to create a line break within a cell that spans multiple columns, but this requires using the `\multicolumn` command to specify the number of columns that the cell should span. For example:

```latex
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
Cell 1 & \multicolumn{2}{c|}{Cell 2} \\ \cline{2-3}
Line 1 of Cell 2 & Line 2 of Cell 2 & Line 3 of Cell 2 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
```

In this example, the `\multicolumn` command is used to specify that the second cell should span two columns, and the `\\` command is used to create a line break within the cell.

